[Music]  nova was an innovation schools for  middle and high school students instead  of having your long classes like math or  english or something we have two  week-long studios and so in these  studios these are projects where we're  presented with an open-ended problem and  almost every project is created with a  team which makes it so much more fun our  pedagogy is based on an architectural  model that is really about a design  process it's teaching critical thinking  it's teaching the ability to empower  your own ideas and go through an  iterative creative process that is  collaborative that it has a critical  loop to it the first couple days we do a  lot of introductory work where the  students get to learn deeply about the  problems that we're addressing and then  we do a lot of brainstorming and  ideation after that phase after the  students divide up into groups I'll go  around to different groups and help them  both by giving feedback about their  designs and their ideas but also  actually helping them technically so  when I was at regular school there was  no chance for me to be myself really  what's really great about newbie is it's  all like what I want to do I'm very  passionate about my projects I'm working  on and I get very interested in them  it's very open and independent and well  most people would think that's a bad  thing kids could just do whatever they  want you're really giving a task here  that it's great when you you're into the  task you really want to just get it done  I came to new because I I like building  things  that the new will be a best fit for me  for building my ideas like making them  come to life and I love regular school  but I thought you'd be very calm really  interested in the engineering and  designing aspect of building and  prototyping and stuff and all this cool  software work so right now I'm working  on a hand drive wheelchair attachment  and so what it does is it uses a  ratcheting mechanism which means it has  free resistance in one direction and  blocks in the other direction but what  we've done is we've stacked two on top  of each other so when you switch the  handle it actually goes backwards I mean  a super cool thing about this which was  like crazy to make was that this one is  actually has a gearing up ratio so it's  faster than width if you would just push  the wheel one to one so this is a one  for the projects that I recently created  which is a music box  that attaches to a bus so the the need  for it is that when you when you sit in  a bus stop obviously everyone's on their  phones and in the winter it's cold and  there's really nothing to do while  waiting for it so the purpose of this  was to create an interactive piece of  art that that brings people together in  a communal way so this is a bus Pole  that everyone sees at the bus stop and  what we created was a cylindrical music  box that attaches to it this is the  music box part there's a resonation  system down here so these are the actual  tongs that are at any given point we  just want to track where the students  are in their process we track the  creative skills and mostly for us this  revolves around Canada critique process  how the students are kind of taking that  critique and synthesizing it into design  decisions how they are communicating  their ideas and how they are  collaborating with others  I think the critique process is  something that is definitely an  adjustment period for some kids and I  think part of it is that like when  you're a creative person when you have  creative ideas it's really hard to  separate them from yourself and so it's  very common for students yet defensive  or to just kind of write off the  critiques just because it's so hard to  like really put yourself out there  and I've definitely had critiques that  have gone really well and I've also had  critiques where like my eyes water a  little bit but I'm also you know I know  that these people are here to help me  like these people are my family at any  of you our hope is that a student goes  away from this experience with the idea  that what they believe in and what they  think matters and that it's really about  the process not about the thing that  comes out the other end I think the most  important piece is really creating  learning environments that engage the  students and you don't engage the  students by giving them problems that  you already know the answer for you know  you engage them by giving problems that  they are excited about that they feel  that they could change the world by  solving them  [Music]  you

